  "verbalization": "Tiapride is associated with Raji through two distinct targets: Inosine-5'-monophosphate dehydrogenase 2 and Valacyclovir hydrolase. Both targets are implicated in the metabolism of nucleoside analogs, which are commonly used in antiviral therapies. The first target, Inosine-5'-monophosphate dehydrogenase 2, is a key enzyme in the de novo nucleotide synthesis pathway, and its inhibition could disrupt viral replication by interfering with nucleotide synthesis. The second target, Valacyclovir hydrolase, is responsible for the breakdown of valacyclovir, an antiviral drug used to treat herpes simplex virus. Inhibition of this enzyme would increase the availability of valacyclovir in the cellular environment, potentially enhancing its antiviral activity.\n\nThe evidence suggests that Tiapride may exert its effects on Raji cells by targeting enzymes involved in nucleotide metabolism and antiviral drug degradation. The presence of two distinct targets, both with a clear biological role in antiviral processes, supports a mechanism of action that involves modulating nucleotide synthesis and enhancing the efficacy of antiviral agents. The integration of these targets into the same biological context, coupled with the presence of a well-characterized mode of action, strengthens the hypothesis that Tiapride interacts with Raji cells through these metabolic pathways.",
